  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThursday-Friday...The surface low reaches central Missouri during \nthe day Thursday as enhanced diffluent flow aloft supports the \nisolated showers and storms (20-30% coverage), mainly south of \nInterstate 80. By Thursday night, another shortwave trough dives\nin from the northwest, but dry air advection is expected to \nsuppress precipitation. By Friday morning, ageostrophic \nconvergence aloft on the backside of the longwave trough now \nover the Great Lakes should lead to a mostly dry Friday. This \nwill be short-lived though as another surface trough moves into \nthe Northern Plains Friday night.\n\nSaturday-Monday...Low PoPs (20-30%) remain on the table heading\ninto the weekend and early next week as continual longwave \ntroughing over Hudson Bay and northwesterly flow aloft over the \nregion may allow for multiple weak shortwave and surface \nboundary passages. A warming trend appears more likely though as\nsoutherly flow ahead of these fronts advect in warm and moist \nair. Highs along and south of Highway 34 are likely (60-80% \nchance per 03.12z LREF guidance) to climb back into low 90s \nSunday and Monday.",
